<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Is Karl-Anthony Towns on a Maximum Deal?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Towns is paid <strong>$57.08m</strong> because the designated veteran rules let a club pay one player 35% of the cap, and he qualified by making an All-NBA team.</p>
<ul>
<li>He was the first pick in 2015 and has never missed a contract year through free agency.</li>
<li>The extension is worth <strong>$220.44m</strong> over four years, fully guaranteed.</li>
<li>It was signed under the designated veteran rule, worth 35% of the cap.</li>
<li>He holds a player option on the final year and a 10% trade bonus.</li>
<li>He is 7 foot and 248 pounds and shoots from three, which is why the money followed.</li>
</ul>
<p>He has been paid by two clubs in twelve seasons and has earned <strong>$343.4m</strong>.</p>

</div><div class="bde-shortcode-506-159 bde-shortcode"><div class="nba-fin" data-nba-fin="why">
